3>Checked Exception vs Unchecked Exception

>“Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler and must be either handled using try-catch or declared using the throws keyword. Unchecked exceptions occur at runtime and are not mandatory to handle. Checked exceptions usually represent recoverable conditions, while unchecked exceptions generally indicate programming errors.”

1. Checked Exception
Examples: IOException, SQLException, ClassNotFoundException
try {
    FileReader file = new FileReader("test.txt");
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("File not found");
}

......................................
2. Unchecked Exception
An unchecked exception is not checked by the compiler. It occurs during runtime, and handling it is not mandatory.

Examples: NullPointerException, ArithmeticException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
int result = 10 / 0;

Main Difference

Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler and must be handled or declared, whereas unchecked exceptions occur at runtime and are not mandatory to handle.
