>>>Multiple Inheritance in Java
answer>>>>“Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes, but it supports multiple inheritance through interfaces.”
>Multiple inheritance means one class inherits from more than one parent class.
class A {
}

class B {
}

class C extends A, B {   // ❌ Not allowed
}
Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes.

>>Why?

Because of the Diamond Problem.

If both A and B have the same method, Java would not know which method C should inherit.
Example:
class C implements A, B
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
Interface vs Abstract Class

>>What is an Abstract Class?
An abstract class is a class that cannot be directly instantiated and can contain both abstract methods and concrete methods.
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

sound() → abstract method
eat() → normal/concrete method
...............
What is an Interface?

An interface is a blueprint that defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.

interface Animal {

    void sound();
}
...........................
interface Animal {

    void sound();
}
A class implements it:

class Dog implements Animal {

    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}


“An abstract class is a partially implemented class that can contain both abstract and concrete methods, whereas an interface defines a contract that a class must implement. Java allows a class to extend only one abstract class but implement multiple interfaces.”
Main Difference
Abstract Class	                                          Interface
Declared using abstract class	                        Declared using interface
Class uses extends	                                       Class uses implements
Can have abstract + concrete methods	                    Mainly used to define a contract
Can have instance variables	                             Variables are public static final by default
A class can extend only one class                  	A class can implement multiple interfaces
Can have constructors	                     Cannot have constructors
